 # Cone Signed Distance Function
 
-Also called: Cone SDF, sdCone, Signed Cone, Signed Capped Cone, Exact Cone SDF, Signed Distance Function (SDF) for Cone
+Also called: Cone SDF, Exact Cone SDF, Signed Capped Cone, Signed Cone, Signed Distance Function (SDF) for Cone, sdCone
 
 ## Overview
-The Cone Signed Distance Function computes the exact Euclidean distance from a query point to the surface of a cone, defined by a base radius and height, with a sign indicating whether the point lies inside or outside the volume [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently rendering or querying conical primitives in ray marching and implicit surface modeling without approximation errors associated with polygonal meshes [S5]. It is distinguished from the [Segment Signed Distance Function](2679-segment-signed-distance-function.md) by its continuous lateral surface and from [Polyhedral Primitives](2529-polyhedral-primitives.md) by its smooth, non-polyhedral geometry [S4, unofficial][S5].
+The Cone Signed Distance Function computes the exact Euclidean distance from a query point to the surface of a cone, defined by a base radius and height, with a sign indicating whether the point lies inside or outside the volume [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. The method evaluates the distance to a cone aligned with the Y-axis [S2, unofficial]. Two primary implementations exist in the literature: an exact geometric projection method that handles the base cap and side slope separately, and a simplified dot-product method often used for infinite or specific capped variants where the base cap is handled implicitly or the cone is defined by a slope vector [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method evaluates the distance to a cone aligned with the Y-axis. Two primary implementations exist in the literature: an "exact" geometric projection method that handles the base cap and side slope separately, and a simplified dot-product method often used for infinite or specific capped variants where the base cap is handled implicitly or the cone is defined by a slope vector.
+The computation operates on scalar values and 2D/3D vectors [S4, unofficial].
 
